Adaptive termination for the HNSW base-layer search, plus oracle instrumentation.

Base:   facebookresearch/faiss v1.15.0 (commit 20f14b31a6d54e243a3d1de6ae193fc4c3ec18ed)
Apply:  cd faiss && git apply 0001-adaptive-termination.patch   (then 0002-traversal-trace.patch, 0003-patience.patch)
Build:  faiss/build_faiss.sh (FAISS_OPT_LEVEL=avx512 for the paper); bench/build_bench.sh links one variant only.

The patch contains four parts.

(1) Adaptive termination. SearchParametersHNSW gains adaptive_gamma and adaptive_k. The base-layer
    loop stops when frontier > (1+gamma)^2 * d_{k_rule} (squared because faiss L2 distances are
    squared). gamma = 0 leaves stock faiss behaviour. When k_rule > k the result heap cannot supply
    d_{k_rule}, so an auxiliary max-heap of the k_rule smallest distances is kept (only while the
    rule is on).

(2) Oracle instrumentation. Records the cumulative base-layer ndis at the moment each target id
    enters the result heap. A true top-K point is never evicted once it enters, so the arrival time
    of the last true neighbour is obtained without re-running the search. Leaving the pointers null
    has no effect. To make the recorded ndis include the distance just computed, the "ndis += ..."
    increments are moved before add_to_heap (ndis is only a counter, so search behaviour is unchanged).

(3) d_k is taken over points that pass the filter (IDSelector), as it is when d_k is read from the
    result heap. Without a filter this is identical. With a filter, the narrower the filter, the
    more conservatively the rule fires.

(4) Filtered search on the unbounded-queue path. Stock v1.15.0 does not pass the selector to this
    path at all, and on the bounded path termination does not depend on the filter, so filtering
    leaves the work unchanged and only recall drops. To get hnswlib-style filtered search both are
    needed:
      (a) selector wiring: navigation (candidates) is unconditional, only results (top_candidates)
          are filtered;
      (b) termination additionally requires top_candidates.size() >= ef: do not stop before the
          result heap is full.
    (a) alone gives the opposite behaviour: with few passing points the "worst" result is close,
    the search stops at once, and a narrower filter does less work. The two go together. Adaptive
    termination and oracle instrumentation are ported to this path too.

(3) and (4) do not change unfiltered search, which is all the paper's experiments use
(bounded queue, no filter).

